---
import ContentPageLayout from '../layouts/ContentPageLayout.astro';
import FeatureCard from '../components/FeatureCard.astro';
import CardDecor from '../components/CardDecor.astro';
import { Trash2, Droplets, AlertTriangle, Layers } from 'lucide-preact';

const title = 'Permanently Delete Text From a PDF | PDkef';
const description =
  'Actually remove text or an image from a PDF instead of covering it with a black box. Learn why drawn rectangles leak, which method to use when, and do it free in your browser.';

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'How is this different from drawing a black box over the text?',
    answer:
      "A drawn rectangle is just another thing painted on the page. The original text is usually still in the file underneath it, and can often be selected, copied, or pulled out with any text-extraction tool. Delete removes the text from the page's instructions entirely, so there is nothing left underneath to recover.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Does deleting one word affect the rest of the page?',
    answer:
      'No. Only what you click is removed. The rest of the page stays real, selectable text rather than being flattened into a picture of itself, the file stays small, and every other page in the document is left byte-for-byte identical.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I delete text from a scanned document?',
    answer:
      "Not with Delete, and this is the one important limitation. In a scan, the words are pixels in a photograph, not text the file knows about, so there is no text object to remove. Use Blackout or Blur on scanned pages instead: both flatten the page afterwards, which genuinely destroys what was underneath.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Does this also remove the information from the document properties?',
    answer:
      "No, and it's worth knowing. Deleting a name from page three doesn't remove it from the document's title, author field, or from page seven if it appears there too. Removing something sensitive means removing every copy of it, wherever it appears.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I undo a deletion before I download the file?',
    answer:
      "Yes. Every deletion goes into the toolbar's undo history, so you can step back at any point before you download. Nothing is written to your actual file until you download it, so the original on your disk is never modified.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Are my PDF files uploaded anywhere?',
    answer:
      'No. This runs entirely in your browser. Your file is never uploaded, never sent over the network, and never stored anywhere outside your own device.',
  },
];
---

<ContentPageLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  breadcrumb="Delete from PDF"
  icon={Trash2}
  kicker="True deletion, not a cover-up"
  h1="Permanently Delete Text From a PDF"
  subhead="Covering something with a black rectangle usually leaves the original text sitting in the file underneath, where anyone can copy it back out. Here's what's actually going on, how to remove content for real, and when a black box is genuinely the right tool anyway."
  primaryCta={{ href: '/redact/', label: 'Delete text from a PDF' }}
  faq={faq}
>
  <FeatureCard density="flow" class="relative isolate overflow-hidden">
    <CardDecor sketch="grid" icon={AlertTriangle} iconPos="tr" iconSize={280} />
    <span class="mb-2 inline-block text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)]">The thing most people get wrong</span>
    <h2 class="mb-4 mt-0 text-[1.6rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-text)]">A black box is a sticker, not an eraser</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 mt-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      It helps to know how a PDF is put together. A page isn't a picture; it's a list of instructions, roughly
      "draw this text here, in this font, at this size." When you drag a black rectangle over a name, you haven't
      edited that list. You've added one more instruction to the end of it: draw a black rectangle here.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4 mt-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      The name is still in the list. It's drawn first and then covered, the way a sticker covers a word in a book. Select
      the area and copy it, and you get the text. Feed the file to any extraction tool and the text comes straight out.
      This is why redaction failures keep making the news, usually in published court filings and government documents
      where the person doing it had every intention of removing the information and reasonably believed they had.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      Nothing about it looks wrong on screen, which is exactly the problem. The document appears finished. The check
      that would catch it, trying to select the text under the box, is one nobody thinks to run.
    </p>
  </FeatureCard>

  <FeatureCard density="flow" variant="highlight" class="relative isolate overflow-hidden">
    <CardDecor sketch="rings" icon={Trash2} iconPos="br" iconSize={280} />
    <span class="mb-2 inline-block text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)]">Three steps</span>
    <h2 class="mb-4 mt-0 text-[1.6rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-text)]">Remove text or an image for real</h2>
    <ol class="m-0 mb-6 flex list-none flex-col gap-5 p-0">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="mt-[0.1rem] flex size-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[var(--color-primary-soft)] text-[0.8rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-primary)]">1</span>
        <span class="text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--color-text)]"><strong class="font-semibold">Open the <a class="text-[var(--color-primary)] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[var(--color-primary-hover)]" href="/redact/">Redact tool</a></strong> and choose or drag in your PDF.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="mt-[0.1rem] flex size-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[var(--color-primary-soft)] text-[0.8rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-primary)]">2</span>
        <span class="text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--color-text)]"><strong class="font-semibold">Pick the Delete tool, then click what you want gone.</strong> Hovering highlights each removable piece of text or image, so you can see exactly what a click will take before you commit to it.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="mt-[0.1rem] flex size-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[var(--color-primary-soft)] text-[0.8rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-primary)]">3</span>
        <span class="text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-[var(--color-text)]"><strong class="font-semibold">Download the PDF, then check your work.</strong> Open it, try to select the area you cleared, and search the document for the word you removed. It should find nothing.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="m-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      Under the hood this rewrites the instructions for that page and leaves every other page in the file untouched.
      The page stays real text rather than becoming an image, so the document remains searchable, selectable, and the
      same size it was. That last step is worth doing anyway, with any tool: verifying takes ten seconds and it's the
      only way to actually know.
    </p>
  </FeatureCard>

  <FeatureCard density="flow" class="relative isolate overflow-hidden">
    <CardDecor sketch="waves" icon={Layers} iconPos="cl" iconSize={280} />
    <span class="mb-2 inline-block text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)]">Pick the right one</span>
    <h2 class="mb-4 mt-0 text-[1.6rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-text)]">Delete, blackout, or blur</h2>
    <p class="mb-6 mt-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      All three are in the same tool and all three are safe, in the sense that none of them leaves recoverable text
      behind. They differ in what they cost you, and the honest answer is that Delete isn't always the right pick.
    </p>
    <div class="mb-6 grid gap-6 grid-cols-3 max-[768px]:grid-cols-1">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="mb-1 flex items-center gap-2 text-[0.95rem] font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">
          <Trash2 size={17} class="text-[var(--color-primary)]" /> Delete
        </div>
        <p class="m-0 text-[0.92rem] leading-[1.55] text-[var(--color-muted)]">
          Removes the content and leaves no gap in its place. The page stays vector text, the file stays small.
          <strong class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Use it for</strong> real text and images in a
          digitally-created PDF. Won't work on a scan.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="mb-1 flex items-center gap-2 text-[0.95rem] font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">
          <Droplets size={17} class="text-[var(--color-primary)]" /> Blackout
        </div>
        <p class="m-0 text-[0.92rem] leading-[1.55] text-[var(--color-muted)]">
          Paints a solid box, then flattens the whole page to an image so nothing survives underneath.
          <strong class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Use it for</strong> scans, and when the reader
          should see that something was withheld.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="mb-1 flex items-center gap-2 text-[0.95rem] font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">
          <Droplets size={17} class="text-[var(--color-primary)]" /> Blur
        </div>
        <p class="m-0 text-[0.92rem] leading-[1.55] text-[var(--color-muted)]">
          Same flattening, softer look. <strong class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Use it for</strong>
          faces, signatures and logos in a screenshot, where a hard black rectangle would be heavier than the
          situation calls for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="m-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      The trade-off is straightforward: Blackout and Blur have to turn the affected page into an image to be safe,
      which makes the file bigger and stops that page's text being searchable. Delete avoids all of that, but only
      reaches content the PDF genuinely knows about. On a scanned form, that's nothing, so blackout is your tool.
    </p>
  </FeatureCard>

  <FeatureCard density="flow" class="relative isolate overflow-hidden">
    <CardDecor sketch="arcs" icon={AlertTriangle} iconPos="tr" iconSize={280} />
    <span class="mb-2 inline-block text-[0.72rem] font-bold uppercase text-[var(--color-primary)]">Before you send it</span>
    <h2 class="mb-4 mt-0 text-[1.6rem] font-bold text-[var(--color-text)]">What removing text doesn't cover</h2>
    <p class="m-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.65] text-[var(--color-text)]">
      Clearing a name off page three does exactly that and nothing more. If the same name appears on page seven, it's
      still on page seven. If it's in the document's title or author properties, filled into a form field, or sitting
      in the filename you're about to attach to an email, none of that is touched either. This isn't a limitation of
      one tool so much as a thing worth carrying into any redaction job: search the finished document for what you
      were trying to remove, and check the parts of a file that aren't the visible page. It takes a minute, and it's
      the difference between a document that looks handled and one that is.
    </p>
  </FeatureCard>
</ContentPageLayout>
